\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Three different commutants for the left Clifford action}

\p{Keep the hypotheses and notation of \ref{fcap-003P}, and let
#{L_x(c)=\delta(x)c}. The internal centralizer #{J\subseteq A},
the right-multiplication operators on #{A}, and the full algebra of
#{\mathfrak g}-module endomorphisms of #{(A,L)} have distinct roles.}

\p{Left multiplication by #{j\in J} commutes with every #{L_x}.
Right multiplication by any #{a\in A} also commutes with every
#{L_x}, purely by associativity. Thus there are injective algebra maps

##{J\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_{\mathfrak g}(A,L),
\qquad j\longmapsto(c\longmapsto jc),}

##{A^{\mathrm{op}}\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_{\mathfrak g}(A,L),
\qquad a\longmapsto(c\longmapsto ca).}

Evaluation at #{1} proves injectivity. The opposite algebra in the
second map records the reversed order of right compositions.}

\p{By \ref{fcap-003G}, #{(A,L)\simeq V_\rho\otimes\mathbb C^m}
with #{m=2^{\ell+r}}. Schur's lemma and the same block calculation as
in \ref{fcap-003P} give

##{\operatorname{End}_{\mathfrak g}(A,L)
\simeq M_m(\mathbb C).}

The dimensions of the three algebras are therefore

##{\dim J=2^\ell,\qquad
\dim A^{\mathrm{op}}=2^{\ell+2r},\qquad
\dim\operatorname{End}_{\mathfrak g}(A,L)=2^{2\ell+2r}.}

For #{\mathfrak g=\mathfrak{sl}_2}, these are #{2}, #{8}, and
#{16}, respectively: #{J\simeq\mathbb C\oplus\mathbb C}, while
#{\operatorname{End}_{\mathfrak g}(A,L)\simeq M_4(\mathbb C)}.
The four-copy left-module calculation is explicit in \ref{fcap-003F}.
For every nonzero complex semisimple #{\mathfrak g}, the dimensions
show that right multiplications form a proper subalgebra of the full
module commutant. They do not by themselves identify that commutant.}
